with multiple files
#[cfg(target_arch = "wasm32")] Configuration attribute. It tells the compiler to completely ignore
or include a block of code based on the target environment. it has arguements like not()

#[derive(...)] — The Macro Workhorse
This is the most common attribute in all of Rust. It tells the compiler
to automatically generate boilerplate traits (interfaces)
for your custom data types so you don't have to write them by hand.

#[must_use] — The Safety Net
This tells the compiler to throw a screaming warning if a developer calls your function
but forgets to actually use or assign the value it returns.

#[repr(...)] — The Memory Architect (Crucial for WASM)
By default, the Rust compiler reserves the right to shuffle the fields of your structs around in memory to optimize space.
But if JavaScript expects your data fields to be in a very strict, predictable linear order, you use this to lock it down.
#[repr(C)] keeps it predictable for layout in memory for bridging or (transparent)

What is #![cfg_attr(...)]?
Think of cfg_attr as a conditional compile statement for other attributes. It essentially tells the compiler:
"If a specific condition is true, automatically apply this other attribute. If it's false, completely ignore it."

#[warn(dead_code || unused variables || clippy::should_implement_trait || deprecated)]
this sends a warning but still compiles

The #[allow()] or #[deny()] allow or deny certain features
#![allow(dead_code)]
#![allow(unused_variables)]

